\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late; Lifted By Stronger Yen Vs US Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 596 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2002\par 11:17\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was higher against its U.S. counterpart late Monday, in line with a stronger yen, but the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  surrendered its early gains due to concern that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be reluctant to allow it to rise too sharply, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0846 GMT (4:46 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.8349, down from S$1.8358 late Friday. It traded between S$1.8320 and S$1.8450. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened lower in Asia with its decline against the yen prompting U.S. investment houses and European banks to sell it against the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened below Y132 and fell to an intraday low of Y131.20 during the session. \par \par \uc2  A large local bank stepped in to buy U.S. dollars after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit its intraday low of S$1.8320, and was joined by Kong Kong-based investment houses, said a dealer at a European bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  News that Dutch electronics giant Philips Electronics will move its regional headquarters from Singapore to Hong Kong, caused worries over Singapore's competitiveness to resurface and market participants feared the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the local dollar from strengthening sharply, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expect the yen to set the tone for the Singapore dollar in overnight trading and at expect it to trade in a S$1.8300-70 range. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds ended mixed late Monday, but all tenors were off their earlier highs in thin trade, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Local bonds opened slightly higher in line with overnight gains in U.S. Treasurys, but surrendered some of their early gains as market participants cautiously adjusted their positions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Not much is being traded, and this is because people don't want to take positions because they expect U.S. interest rates to rise later this year," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "But for now the fact that the U.S. rates are expected to remain on hold till June is generally supporting bond prices," the dealer added. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds was down two basis points at 1.78%, while the yield on five-year bonds was flat at 3.21%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds was flat at 3.75% while the yield on 10-year bonds fell one basis point to 4.02%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds was flat at 4.49%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, the most liquid seven-year swap\par offer fell one basis point to 3.86%. The 10-year swap offer was flat at\par 4.17%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps widened to 199.5 basis\par points from 197 basis points late Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the local money market, rates were mostly steady as liquidity\par remained sufficient in the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Monday       Late Friday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.5000% 0.6250%   0.3750% 0.6250%\par 3-Month           0.8125% 0.9375%   0.8125% 0.9375%\par 1-Year            1.2500% 1.3750%   1.2500% 1.3750%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-64154-152;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020408dy48000ni\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
