\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Briefly Hits 11-Week Low Of S$1.8507 Vs Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 529 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2002\par 06:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar briefly fell to an 11-week low of S$1.8507 against its U.S. counterpart Wednesday, before regaining its footing after local banks stepped in to sell U.S. dollars, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar had touched a high of S$1.8500 in overnight trading in New York. It started the Asian trading day lower around S$1.8490 against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but up from S$1.8428 late Tuesday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Aggressive U.S. dollar buying by U.S. investment banks to cover their short positions pushed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above the key psychological level of S$1.8500 in early Asian trades. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar last traded above S$1.8500 Jan. 10, when it touched a high of S$1.8510. Before that, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had risen to an 11-year high of S$1.8550 Dec. 28, 2001. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's rise above S$1.8500 prompted a large local bank to sell the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pushing it down by around 30 cents to S$1.8475. This sparked fears that the bank may have been acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It's hard to say (if it was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )," a European bank dealer said. "That local bank may have just been selling to take profit, but it's natural for the market to think this could be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It can't be confirmed," he added. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar has since pulled back further and was quoted at S$1.8467 around 0508 GMT (00:08 a.m. EST). Dealers said the U.S. dollar was unlikely to retest S$1.8500 locally again Wednesday, as market participants are wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  According to a JPMorgan Chase research report, a combination of superficially weak industrial production numbers, and the recent liberalization of Singapore dollar lending rules by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "encouraged a bout of unwinding of short U.S. dollar/Singapore dollar positions, primarily by international investors." \par \par \uc2  Data issued Tuesday showed that industrial production fell 11.3% year-on-year, compared with a 4.3% increase in January, although this was due mainly to a seasonal effect of having fewer working days in the month because of holidays. \par \par \uc2  JPMorgan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist James Malcolm said suspected selling of U.S. dollars on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggests the MAS is reluctant to allow the local dollar to slide into the weak range of its undisclosed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy band. \par \par \uc2  "This makes sense as officials are likely to want some flexibility in its policy management regime should dollar/sing spike higher on any dollar/yen near-term rally," he said. \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, JPMorgan said improving domestic economic fundamentals are likely to continue supporting the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Malcolm recommends selling U.S. dollars as he expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to retreat to S$1.7900 by the end of September and S$1.7800 by the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  -By Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-64154-157; izham.ahmad@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020327dy3r000co\par }\page {\par\fs20{\