\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; HKD Dlr Wet Blanket On Mkt \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2 1,032 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2002\par 10:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed late Friday, after largely relinquishing their earlier gains as renewed jitters over the Hong Kong dollar's fate overwhelmed the support from a strong Japanese yen and growing optimism of a U.S.-led economic recovery, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's markedly more upbeat assessment of the U.S. economy Thursday, compared with his comments made just last week, failed to sustain the recent upward momentum in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "You would expect good sentiment on global reflation because of Greenspan's speech last night and the strength of the yen to help Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2  Instead, anxieties about the Hong Kong dollar peg and the unwinding of short yen positions against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tossed a wet blanket on the market, he added. \par \par \uc2  The risk premium on holding one-year Hong Kong dollar forward shot up to 225-270 to the spot rate, compared with 160-170 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers blamed the rise in Hong Kong dollar forward premiums to a report issued recently by U.S.-based consultancy Medley Global Advisors, questioning the future of the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The report stoked nervousness about a depegging of the Hong Kong dollar, in turn re-igniting fears of a competitive devaluation in Asia. Analysts, however, said that such prospects didn't appear imminent. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. dollar was more biddish against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of the Hong Kong dollar peg rumor," said a senior dealer at a European bank. "Everybody just jumped onto the bandwagon; nobody wanted to be caught short in dollar/Asians." \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar retreated past the key S$1.8200 level against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , from its intraday high of S$1.8170. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday, the Singapore dollar was slightly weaker at S$1.8213, compared with S$1.8208 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The yen's continued rally overnight had propelled the Singapore dollar to as high as S$1.8135. \par \par \uc2  After posting its biggest single-day gain against the dollar in three-and-a-half years Thursday, the dollar was quoted at Y127.72 at 0920 GMT Friday, above Y126.99 in New York but well below Y129.91 in Tokyo late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The yen has appreciated over 5.5% against the dollar the first four days of this week, climbing by 3.4% Thursday alone. \par \par \uc2  Recent U.S.-dollar purchases by a large \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  bank left the market wondering if it was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the strength of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in check. \par \par \uc2  But Jimmy Koh, the head of treasury research at Singapore's UOB Group, said such fears were "unwarranted." \par \par \uc2  Based on the bank's in-house model of the MAS' policy band, the upside limit on the Singapore dollar "could stretch to S$1.8040" based on the current levels of the yen and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Koh said. \par \par \uc2  He said that with the yen in stronger territory, the Singapore dollar will likely trade between S$1.8100 and S$1.8300 for now. The U.S. dollar's rise to S$1.8250-S$1.8300 will provide opportunities for participants to get out of their long positions in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he added. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won ended barely changed against the U.S. dollar, but weaker against its Japanese counterpart - which continued to outperform the rest of the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Late dollar purchases by offshore participants and importers in South Korea pared the won's earlier gains, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Expectations of increased dollar demand from foreign investors, who dumped some KRW230 billion worth of South Korean shares Friday, also contributed to the won's pullback against the dollar, Mohi-uddin at UBS Warburg said. \par \par \uc2  He added that foreign investors were selling shares of South Korean and Taiwanese companies, and shifting their funds into the Tokyo stock market, which has recently been rallying. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at KRW1,314.3, steady compared with KRW1,314.5 Thursday. The dollar hit an intraday low of KRW1,306.5 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The yen was quoted around KRW10.29, higher than KRW10.17 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Propped up by the yen, the New Taiwan dollar finished at a two-month closing high against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep a rein on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation, buying an estimated US$250 million to US$300 million in the market, dealers in Taipei said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$34.947, down from NT$34.989 Thursday. The U.S. dollar hit an intraday low of NT34.886. Dealings were valued at US$634 million. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was marginally stronger at 43.250 baht to the dollar, compared with THB43.275 late Thursday, but was off its new one-year high of THB43.110 reached early Friday. \par \par \uc2  Despite the baht's recent appreciation, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula said Friday the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "is not too strong," and that the current level is still competitive for the country's exports. \par \par \uc2  He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to monitor the baht's performance, but didn't make it clear if it had bought dollars in the market recently - as suspected by \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers. \par \par \uc2  The central banker's comments failed to push the baht higher. Instead, the baht retreated on worries about the future of the Hong Kong dollar peg, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  On the Philippine Dealing System, the dollar ended at 50.97 pesos, relatively steady compared with PHP50.99 Thursday. The dollar opened at its intraday low of PHP50.90, but spiked toward PHP51 toward the close. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah retreated to IDR10,035 against the dollar, compared with IDR9,945 late Thursday, as local corporations snapped up the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  following its slide to a fresh five-month low of IDR9,890 earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4043; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020308dy38000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
