\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Up Late; Recover Along With Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2 1,061 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2002\par 11:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were stronger late Tuesday, as the Japanese yen's continued recovery offset the market's somber contemplation over the prospects of a U.S. economic recovery, observers said. \par \par \uc2  The yen's rise gained momentum after China issued its strongest warning yet over the yen's recent downdraft to three-year lows. \par \par \uc2  At 0900 GMT (4:00 a.m. EST), the dollar was quoted at Y131.20, below Y132.01 late Monday in New York. The yen hit a new three-year low of Y133.40 last Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  While reaffirming the country's pledge to maintain the stability of the yuan, People's Bank of China Governor Dai Xianglong warned that a further depreciation of the yen would increase the pressure on the yuan exchange rate and called on Tokyo to maintain the yen's stability. \par \par \uc2  While Dai ruled out China devaluing the yuan for now, he mentioned the risk of a weakening yen leading to "a domino effect of depreciations in Asia." \par \par \uc2  South Korea, too, reiterated its concerns over the yen. The country's Finance and Economy Minister Jin Nyum said Tuesday the government is currently collaborating with other neighboring countries to address "\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate concerns," notably the weak yen, according to Yonhap news agency. He warned that it would be a "disaster" for the world economy if Japan moves to reflate its ailing economy through a weak yen policy. \par \par \uc2  Their expressions of concern over the yen came at the end of Japanese Prime Minister Juniro Koizumi week-long Southeast Asian tour, during which Japanese monetary officials have toned down their weaker yen rhetoric. Market observers now expect Tokyo to resume talking down the yen - albeit less aggressively for the short term. \par \par \uc2  "Generally, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are in a correction phase together with the yen," said a senior dealer at a European bank. "We will see where dollar/yen will move from here, but Y130 will provide a very strong psychological support." \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar surged as market participants trimmed their long U.S. dollar positions, triggering stop-loss orders along the way, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "There was long-liquidation by U.S. and U.K. funds," said a dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  He warned, however, that the selling in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was overdone, and could provoke \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has, however, appeared to tolerate the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's movements - at least for now. \par \par \uc2  "Based on the Singapore dollar trade-weighted index, it seems to me that the Singapore dollar is a bit too strong," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar skidded to S$1.8253, down sharply from S$1.8383 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht's gains lagged behind those of the Singapore dollar and yen. \par \par \uc2  There were suspicions that the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was checking the baht's strength, in part to maintain the competitiveness of the country's exports, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers believe the Bank of Thailand has been buying dollars daily since late last week via a Japanese bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Late in the day, the dollar was fetching 43.870 baht, down marginally from THB43.900 late Monday. For most of the day, the dollar traded above THB43.900 - a level that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to have supported in recent sessions. \par \par \uc2  Apparently concerned over the competitiveness of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to mitigate the upward pressure on the New Taiwan dollar from the yen's gains, foreign equity fund inflows and U.S. dollar sales by exporters, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought an estimated US$200 million during the day, a foreign bank dealer in Taipei estimated. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.999 - below NT$35.027 Monday - but off Tuesday's intraday low of NT$34.967. Trading volume was US$520 million, almost double that of US$282 million Monday. \par \par \uc2  On the Philippine Dealing System, the dollar ended at 51.255 pesos, down from PHP51.345 Monday. \par \par \uc2  Bucking the trend in the region, the South Korean won succumbed to heavy foreign equity fund outflows, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  As the won's movements decoupled from the yen's, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovered above the psychologically important KRW10.00 mark for the first time in a week. The dollar was fetching KRW10.05 late Tuesday, up from KRW9.94 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. funds were covering their yen/won positions - they've gone short in the cross since the end of last year and early this year," said a Singapore dealer. \par \par \uc2  Against the U.S. dollar, the won finished at KRW1,316.9, weaker than Monday's close at KRW1,312.3. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors dumped some KRW294.2 billion in shares, sending the benchmark stock index reeling by 3.4% to a close of 718.64 points, its lowest finish since the end of last year. \par \par \uc2  Market participants who had bought \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the won, on hopes that the country is well-positioned to gain from a U.S. economic rebound, unwound their positions as such optimism faded, observers said. \par \par \uc2  "There was an unwinding of these reflation trades that were put on at the end of last year and early this year," said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's cautious comments on the economy at the end of last week served as a reality check on the market, dashing hopes for a speedy U.S. economic recovery. In his downbeat assessment on the U.S. economy, Greenspan had emphasized the risks to growth and corporate profits. \par \par \uc2  Adding to the somber mood in Seoul's markets, South Korean Finance and Economy Minister Jin Nyum said Tuesday that it would be difficult for the country to see a genuine recovery in exports and capital investment during the first half of 2002, according to Yonhap News Agency. But he added that the government will nevertheless seek to attain a "reasonable" economic growth rate in the first half through fiscal and financial policies. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was steady around 10,390 rupiah to the dollar, compared with IDR10,400 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4043; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020115dy1f000lg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
