\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Weaker Late As Yen Suffers Relapse \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2 896 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2002\par 10:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The yen's relapse to a 39-month low flung Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the weaker end of their recent ranges late Tuesday, ending their recent reprieve. \par \par \uc2  But the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' decline continued to trail behind that of the yen. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of export-driven economies - in particular the South Korean won, New Taiwan dollar and the Singapore dollar - will likely continue to outperform the yen in coming months, amid expectations that they will be well positioned to gain from a U.S. economic convalescence. \par \par \uc2  "The problem is a Japanese problem; it's not an Asian problem," said a dealer at a European bank. "Now it's just a question of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' competitiveness vis-a-vis dollar/yen." \par \par \uc2  Market watchers said there's a growing risk that the yen could slide past Y135 to the U.S. dollar, as Japanese officials appear to endorse a further weakening of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Renewed faith in Tokyo's tolerance for a weaker yen battered the yen to as low Y132.80 against the U.S. dollar - its weakest level since Oct. 6, 1998. At 0915 GMT (4:15 a.m. EST), the U.S. dollar was fetching Y132.54. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman, Vice-Finance Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda, said early Tuesday that "current (market) conditions reflect a correction," from a previously too-strong yen, suggesting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had more room to weaken. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka said, after a meeting with U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill Monday, that "exchange rates should be left to market forces, but I don't think the current level doesn't reflect Japan's economic fundamentals." \par \par \uc2  Still, with \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  participants holding long U.S. dollar positions against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yen would need to drop much further before they would be enticed to push regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  beyond their recent lows. \par \par \uc2  "Maybe if dollar/yen goes to Y135 then everybody would sell Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  again," said a senior dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  Fears that monetary authorities in Asia won't tolerate excessive volatility in their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have also restrained U.S. dollar bulls, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "For now, we think most of the authorities are still comfortable with the yen's level," said Philip Wee, a market strategist at DBS Bank. "What they don't want is volatility from the yen." \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar's movements, in particular, have been relatively tame despite the yen's gyrations, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent move to narrow the policy band within which it guides the trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar has stoked fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The market doesn't dare speculate against the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar too much for the time being," said a dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was quoted at S$1.8495 against its U.S. counterpart, weaker than S$1.8407 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  In Seoul, the U.S. dollar closed at 1,309.5 won, up from Monday's close of KRW1,302.0. \par \par \uc2  The yen remained depressed below KRW10.00, despite the market's belief that South Korean authorities would prefer to keep the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above that level to maintain the price competitiveness of South Korea's exports. With the South Korean economy expected to outshine Japan's, the yen was fetching KRW9.88. \par \par \uc2  U.S. dollar sales by foreign investors, who had snapped up South Korean shares in recent sessions, also kept a rein on the won's slide, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. dollar closed at NT$35.038, higher than NT$34.943 Monday, in dealings valued at US$567 million. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't appear too alarmed by the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline as it was stemmed by foreign portfolio inflows and was in line with the weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region, especially the won, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market - sold around US$150 million, but only to cushion the New Taiwan dollar's slide, dealers in Taipei said. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened to 44.070 baht to the U.S. dollar, compared with THB43.795 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Thailand Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula said Tuesday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't use the baht as a tool to stimulate exports, signaling that it won't tolerate a further weakening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exchange rate policy must balance the need to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves strong to meet external debt repayments and the need to support export growth at the same time. \par \par \uc2  However, Pridiyathorn said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't want to allow the baht to strengthen to the point at which it could hurt exports either, and that's why it decided last month to cut its benchmark interest rate. \par \par \uc2  On the Philippine Dealing System, the pressure on the peso from the yen was somewhat mitigated by persistent inflows of U.S. dollar remittances from Filipinos working overseas, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at 51.680 pesos, up from PHP51.500 Monday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Indonesian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakened to 10,420 rupiah to the U.S. dollar,\par compared with IDR10,390 late Monday.\par    -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4043;\par netty.ismaildowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020108dy18000i8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
