\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Asian  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  expected to cruise in calm waters. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jennifer Chen \par \uc2 740 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2002\par 09:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 8 (Reuters) - Punters should be rubbing their hands in glee at the prospect of more volatility in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as 2002 kicked off with the yen bumping into fresh three-year lows. \par \par \uc2  But that's been far from the case as interest from speculators towards the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains weak because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  habits of regional central banks, said analysts and dealers. \par \par \uc2  "Every \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia manages its exchange rate very closely with the view of damping volatility," said Vincent Low, fixed income and regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Merrill Lynch in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the yen and the much anticipated U.S. recovery - last year's dominating factors in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets - will only fuel central banks' determination to head off volatility. \par \par \uc2  The weaker yen will prompt regional central banks, particularly in North Asia, to limit their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' upsides to maintain export competitiveness. But they will still be keen to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from weakening too much as they wait for an economic rebound in the United States to take definite shape. \par \par \uc2  GLOBAL RECOVERY AWAITED \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen will put a cap on how much the won and Taiwan dollar can move up, and it's only when a global recovery is firmly entrenched that the Korean and Taiwan central banks will step out of the way," said James Malcolm, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, market players say the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's recent decision to narrow the trading band for the trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar reflected a willingness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more frequently in the market. \par \par \uc2  "There's not going to be the same degree of flexibility as they showed last year," said the head dealer at a foreign bank in Hong Kong, who added that he expected other Asian central banks to act the same way. \par \par \uc2  The MAS uses its trade-weighted Sing dollar to guide monetary policy. As a reaction to the city-state's severe economic downturn last year, the bank widened the trading band for the trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Analysts say its decision to reverse that move reflects growing confidence over the economy. \par \par \uc2  Market players dubbed the yen and long-awaited U.S. recovery, now expected for the second half of this year, as the sources of possible volatility for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  While they unanimously agree that the markets will react violently if the U.S. recovery fails to materialise, they disagree over the extent to which a weaker yen will impact Asia. \par \par \uc2  Still, more and more people in the market are arriving upon the view that while the yen will determine the overall trend for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , its influence was dwindling. The yen might fall to fresh three-year lows, but it will take some doing for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to see their own record lows. \par \par \uc2  Even with Japanese authorities clearly advocating a weaker yen for the country to export itself out of trouble, analysts say an apocalyptic meltdown in the yen was unlikely because that would provoke strong objections from Japan's neighbours, especially China and Korea, which have already vocalised concerns at the end of last year. \par \par \uc2  "Nobody in Asia wants to give Japan an inch," said JP Morgan Chase's Malcolm, who has an end-June forecast for the yen of 138 to the dollar, a far cry from Asian financial crisis lows of nearly 150. \par \par \uc2  DELAYED CATCH-UP \par \par \uc2  Analysts also noted that the yen's current downtrend may amount to a long-delayed catch-up with the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Since the Asian financial crisis, a lot of these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost a huge amount of their value, and now the yen is finally catching up. You can say it's been overvalued all these years," said Thio Chin Loo, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Furthermore, North Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been enjoying a degree of stability thanks to the flood of capital inflows that began last year. Analysts say it's telling that the won, though weaker, is still outperforming the yen as reflected by the yen/won cross-rate, which has dipped to levels not seen since July 25, 1999. \par \par \uc2  To add insult to injury, even notoriously volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Indonesian rupiah and Philippine peso are expected to enjoy a more stable year compared with the yen because of the absence of their traditional foe - political instability. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020108dy1800i7o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
