\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Asia: MAS May Put Brakes On Singapore Dollar Skid \b0\par\par\uc2  By Izham Ahmad \par \uc2 806 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2001\par 08:17\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Singapore's economy is looking decidedly weak - indeed, the government thinks this year could see the nation's worst economic performance since independence in 1965. \par \par \uc2  So is this a good time to sell the local dollar? Even at 11-year lows the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  arguably has room to weaken further. After all, this country's economy relies heavily on earnings from exports, which are as vulnerable as ever amid the global economic and security uncertainty. \par \par \uc2  A weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  might ameliorate the problem to some extent. \par \par \uc2  There's just one problem: The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore probably doesn't feel all that inclined to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slide much further. Many analysts suspect the local dollar has neared the limit - undisclosed by MAS - where \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  becomes ever more likely. \par \par \uc2  The MAS allows the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to trade against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of major trading partners within a band. The list of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that make up the basket is confidential, although it isn't difficult to imagine that the dollar, euro and yen are the major components. \par \par \uc2  Far more difficult is gauging the boundaries of the band - also a closely guarded secret. \par \par \uc2  A major pastime of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts here is to make educated estimates about the band's perimeters. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this week, as the U.S. dollar shot through key psychological resistance at S$1.8400 en route to an 11-year high of S$1.8430, the MAS remained sidelined, offering little opposition, verbal or otherwise, to the move. \par \par \uc2  Based on that, many now estimate that the top of band lies somewhere between S$1.8450 and S$1.8500, but no one really has more than a hunch. \par \par \uc2  Around 0700 GMT (2:00 a.m. EST), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.8367, after having retraced somewhat on fears of MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  As ever, planning \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy here requires powers of deduction worthy of Sherlock Holmes. \par \par \uc2  The latest round of Singapore dollar declines comes after the MAS announced last month that it would widen the trading band to cope with expected increased volatility in the wake of the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks in the U.S. and the ensuing conflict in Afghanistan. \par \par \uc2  Analysts took the wider band to mean that MAS would allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken a bit more than previously was the case. \par \par \uc2  The last time the MAS is believed to have aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in July, halting the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar slide at its previous 11-year low of S$1.8398. That was before the band was widened. \par \par \uc2  Based on this information, analysts say the upper tier of the newly widened band probably isn't much higher for the U.S. dollar than current levels. \par \par \uc2  "Even if you widen the band by 1.5 percentage points (on each side of the pivot point), you'll be looking at S$1.8460-70 as the limits. This is purely a 'guesstimate,' but anything higher than S$1.8450 could mean the limits of the band," said Jimmy Koh, head Of treasury research at United Overseas Bank Ltd. \par \par \uc2  Stealth is crucial for MAS. Ideally, it is uncertainty over the band alone that eventually slows \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves in either direction. This saves the MAS from having to directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \uc2  Irene Cheung, sovereign and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN-Amro Bank, says she estimates the MAS band's limits to be around S$1.8450-S$1.8500. \par \par \uc2  "We're not far off from there. But as long as they (MAS) don't show their hand we won't know for sure where that band is," Cheung said. \par \par \uc2  Even though many are reluctant to test the resolve of MAS by pushing the U.S. dollar much higher, they aren't eager to sell the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  either. \par \par \uc2  Most expect MAS to maintain its current neutral monetary policy - inviting neither gains nor losses by the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the basket - until the middle of next year. \par \par \uc2  The next policy review is scheduled in January. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar has been rejuvenated by recent developments in Afghanistan as well as by a slew of highly encouraging economic data which seem to hint the economy may be ready to stabilize soon. \par \par \uc2  The persistently gloomy outlook for the Japanese economy has also boosted the U.S. dollar against its Asian counterparts as Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tend to take their lead from the yen. \par \par \uc2  "We expect the U.S. dollar to be generally well underpinned going forward especially on further optimism over the U.S. economy and the war on terrorism," DBS Bank Research said recently. \par \par \uc2  -By Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4150; izham.ahmad@dowjones.com \par \pa