\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Ctrl Bks Cap Won, TWD Gains \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 771 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2001\par 10:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed late Friday, with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central banks in South Korea and Taiwan keeping a rein on the continued strengthening of their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese yen's overnight bounce gave an added fillip to the South Korean won and the New Taiwan dollar, both of which have propped up by foreign portfolio inflows in recent weeks. In contrast, their Southeast Asian counterparts had wobbled on economic and geopolitical anxieties. \par \par \uc2  The yen rose as \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  participants sold the euro for the yen. However, the market welcomed the half percentage point cut in interest rates by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday. \par \par \uc2  At 0815 GMT (3:15 a.m. EST), the dollar was quoted at Y120.22, above Y119.99 late Thursday in New York. The dollar was at Y121.00 late Thursday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  As foreign funds investing in South Korean shares continued to sell dollars, the won extended its recent ascent to end at a nine-week high, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in an apparent attempt to prevent the U.S. dollar from falling below 1,280 won, bought the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pulling the won - which has appreciated by about 1% in the past week - off its high, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A state-run bank started to buy dollars when the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit an intraday low of KRW1,280.2; dealers believed the transactions were executed on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at KRW1,283.1, lower than KRW1,281.1 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors bought a net KRW206.9 billion in shares Friday, helping to lift the benchmark stock index by 0.7%. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  signalled its resolve to prevent the local dollar from strengthening past NT$34.500 against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying an estimated US$100 million to US$200 million in the market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at its intraday high of NT$34.500, slightly lower than Thursday's NT$34.510. \par \par \uc2  U.S.-dollar sales by banks and exporters had pushed the New Taiwan dollar to as high as NT$34.455 in the morning session, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widely expected to keep the New Taiwan dollar steady, and manage a gradual appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the Dec. 1 legislative elections approach, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But the New Taiwan dollar will likely weaken after the elections to reflect the island state's ailing economic fundamentals, market watchers said. \par \par \uc2  Uninspired by the yen's gains, the Singapore dollar slumped on short-covering of U.S. dollar positions by interbank participants, dealers said. Singapore banks were also believed to be buying U.S. dollars on behalf of corporate clients, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was also roiled by the Asian Development Bank's comments, in a report issued Friday, that the Monetary of Singapore might have to actively prevent the Singapore dollar from appreciating to cushion the slide in the city state's exports, Bank of America said in a note to clients. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was at S$1.8265, higher than S$1.8169 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Last-minute U.S. dollar purchases, believed to be by oil importers, pushed the Philippine peso into negative territory toward the close, dealers said. The peso was steady most of the day on seasonal remittance inflows from overseas Filippino workers, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at its intraday high of 51.915 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP51.750 Thursday. It traded to as low as PHP51.720 earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  Despite a denial by the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some dealers believed it may have bought U.S. dollars to replenish its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  extended its rebound of the past few days to trade around 10,400 rupiah to the U.S. dollar, compared with IDR10,480 late Thursday, on suspected U.S. dollar sales by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar briefly pierced through IDR11,000 Tuesday - for the first time since President Megawati Sukarnoputri took office July 23 - as the slow pace of economic reforms in Indonesia continues to try the market's patience. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been defending the rupiah since then. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was marginally weaker at 44.495 baht to the U.S. dollar, compared with THB44.480 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4043;\par netty.ismail@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011109dxb9000k7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex