\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Dn Late On Yen; SGD Hardest Hit \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 871 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2001\par 11:24\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  floundered late Thursday as the yen slumped past Y123 to the dollar, and concerns over the region's deteriorating fundamentals continued to loom in the background, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won and the New Taiwan dollar largely held their own, as persistent foreign equity fund inflows into their respective stock markets negated the pressure from the yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0853 GMT (3:53 a.m. EST), the dollar was quoted at Y123.26, above Y122.90 late Wednesday in New York. The dollar was at Y122.60 late Wednesday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "The market is still keen to buy dollars on any dip, in tandem with the global dollar picture," said a dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was the hardest hit in Asia, temporarily sinking past S$1.8300 against its U.S. counterpart to hit an intraday low of S$1.8325 in earlier trading - its weakest level since July 19. \par \par \uc2  The weaker yen, euro and rupiah led participants to again probe the boundaries of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's widened policy band, pushing the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Jitters surrounding the Nov. 3 general election - to be held in the midst of the city-state's worst recession in its 36-year history - had also cast a shadow on the Singapore dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Goh Chok Tong's announcement Thursday that Finance Minister Richard Hu will retire and won't stand for the coming election provided participants with an added "excuse" to sell down the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said a dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  Still, policy continuity will likely be assured, as Health Minister Lim Hng Kiang, who's also second minister for finance, is widely expected to take over Hu's position. \par \par \uc2  When it became clear that the long-ruling People's Action Party, or PAP, was returned to power when nominations closed Thursday, the Singapore dollar recovered with the U.S. unit falling below the S$1.8300 level, dealers said. Opposition parties named candidates for just 29 of 84 seats in Parliament. \par \par \uc2  Fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  also helped the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regain its footing, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "There were rumors that the authority was checking prices when the U.S. dollar was above S$1.8300," said a senior dealer at a European bank. "That made the market nervous and triggered some long liquidation." \par \par \uc2  But some dealers doubted the veracity of such rumors. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen is at Y123," said a dealer at another European bank. "There's no reason for the MAS to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  The market took the cue from a large local bank, which had sold the U.S. dollar when it traded around S$1.8320, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Late Thursday, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.8277, still higher than S$1.8262 late Wednesday, as the market's voracious appetite for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to keep it afloat. \par \par \uc2  Intermittent light dollar sales by state banks, believed to be acting on behalf of the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , mitigated the pressure on the rupiah, which has been bruised by corporate demand for dollars, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 10,250 rupiah, higher than IDR10,230 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slump, month-end corporate demand for dollars and security concerns, sparked by rumors of coup plots against the Philippine government, conspired to send the peso sliding past the psychologically important PHP52 mark to the dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The peso closed at PHP52.010, its weakest level since Aug. 9, compared with PHP51.945 Wednesday. Volume rose to $100 million from $85.3 million Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely sold as much as $50 million in the morning session, but stopped its dollar sales when it realized it was futile to fight the rising tide of corporate dollar demand. \par \par \uc2  However, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Rafael Buenaventura denied the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday. \par \par \uc2  As it attempted to play catch-up with its weaker regional peers, the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's technical support at 44.850 baht per dollar finally gave way, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at THB44.880, higher than THB44.765 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  North Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to outperform their Southeast Asian peers, as foreign equity funds continued to flood into their respective stock markets, countering the onslaught from the yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought U.S. dollars to curb the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at NT$34.545, unchanged from Wednesday. Dealings were valued at US$273 million. \par \par \uc2  Foreign institutional investors bought a net NT$3.63 billion worth of Taiwanese shares Thursday, the Taiwan Stock Exchange said. \par \par \uc2  In Seoul, the dollar finished at 1,297.0 won, after trading in a tight KRW2-range, marginally higher than KRW1,296.0 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors bought a net KRW110.8 billion in Korean shares, extending their buying into an 11th straight session. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4043; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dxap09ph9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
