\par\par \b \uc2  US Dlr Above S$1.83; First Time Since July 23 - Dealers \b0\par\par\uc2 225 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2001\par 03:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar fell through a key support level early Thursday as the U.S. dollar rose above S$1.83 for the first time since July, amid gains by the U.S. unit against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0140 GMT, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.8308 from S$1.8257 late Wednesday. It has since reached a high of S$1.8313. \par \par \uc2  This is the first time since July 23, that the U.S. dollar has traded above S$1.8300, according to dealers. \par \par \uc2  "Right now all the U.S. names are buying," said a dealer in a Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  The buying had been triggered by overnight gains in the U.S. dollar against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially the yen. \par \par \uc2  However, market participants are treading cautiously at current levels as some fear the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilize any sudden volatility in the local market. \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, dealers said the overall sentiment remains bullish on the U.S. dollar against most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  Next resistance for the U.S. dollar is expected at S$1.8350, while support is expected at S$1.8270. \par \par \uc2  -By Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4150; izham.ahmad@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dxap00552\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
