\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Singapore Dlr At 11-Week Low \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 831 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2001\par 11:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed late Thursday, with the Singapore dollar extending its slide to 11-week lows as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently acquiesced to a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Foreign equity inflows helped support the South Korean won and New Taiwan dollar, which have recently been outperforming their Southeast Asian peers, burdened by economic and security anxieties, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah and Philippine peso were firmer as their respective central banks mounted a continued defense of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which threaten to take a dive as domestic concerns persist. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared determined to prevent the dollar from rising beyond 51.800 pesos, while dealers believed its Indonesian counterpart was keen to keep the dollar below 10,000 rupiah. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht was slightly lower, but, given the lack of trading interest in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , continued to trail behind the Singapore dollar's decline, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  While the other Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  held their own, the Singapore dollar, being the most liquid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , took the brunt of the region's woes. \par \par \uc2  Dealing another blow to the Singapore dollar was a New York Times article Wednesday that terrorists linked to Osama bin Laden's al-Qaeda group based in the Philippines, Indonesia and Malaysia were "likely the targets of future covert and overt American actions," dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar slumped past S$1.8100 overnight to as low as S$1.8160 against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The Singapore dollar is used as a negative hedge," given capital restrictions in Malaysia, Philippines, Indonesia which limit the availability of dollars in these countries, said an Asian bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  In response to the report, however, Secretary of State Colin Powell said Wednesday that the U.S. is concentrating on its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  against Afghanistan and any actions against other nations are for the more distant future. U.S. ambassador to Singapore, Frank Lavin, added Thursday that U.S. military action in Southeast Asia is unlikely. \par \par \uc2  Without any evidence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the local \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar extended its precipitous decline to an 11-week low of S$1.8251 against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday, down from S$1.8068 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar has floundered on concerns over the severity of the city-state's economic slump and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to widen its policy band to give it more flexibility in managing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Singapore dollar is guided within an undisclosed trading band against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the country's major trading partners. \par \par \uc2  Market participants continued to push the Singapore dollar lower, as they attempted to gauge the limits of the MAS' widened policy band, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Persistent U.S. dollar purchases by a large Singapore bank, which helped thrust the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through S$1.8200 Thursday, led some in the market to surmise that it was acting on behalf of the MAS. Some dealers, however, doubted such talk. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore bank turned a seller of U.S. dollars in late trading, causing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to retreat, but only temporarily. \par \par \uc2  Heavy dollar sales by exporters and a 2.7% jump on the Seoul bourse - following solid gains on Wall Street overnight - lifted the South Korean won, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 1,302.0 won, down from Wednesday's close of KRW1,306.7. \par \par \uc2  After leaving its call rate unchanged, South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hinted that its recent rate cuts might be sufficient for now, saying that the rate "had already been lowered considerably." \par \par \uc2  "After the rate decision, we believe that the won should be weakening from here," Bank of America said. "There has to be some stimulus from somewhere. The economy would certainly worsen if bond rates continue to rise and the won continues to strengthen." \par \par \uc2  In Taipei, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought an estimated US$20 million toward the close to counter a strengthening of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the 4.7% rally in the stock market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.553, little changed from NT$34.555 Tuesday. The market was closed Wednesday for a national holiday. \par \par \uc2  On the Philippine Dealing System, dealers believed the peso continued to find reprieve from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  One Manila-based dealer estimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold around $50 million at PHP51.800. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at its intraday low of PHP51.750, from PHP51.791 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Volume rose to $75 million from $51.7 million Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The market shrugged off the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's expected decision to leave its closely-watched overnight rates unchanged. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was stronger at 9,955 rupiah per dollar, compared with IDR10,005 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was marginally weaker at 44.785 baht to the dollar, compared with IDR44.755 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4043; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011011dxab000m5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
