\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Down Late; Hits 2-Mo Low On Weak Yen,Rupiah \b0\par\par\uc2 621 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2001\par 10:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar fell to its lowest level in nearly two months Tuesday, as a weaker yen and Indonesian rupiah triggered stop-loss U.S. dollar buying by banks and investment houses, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0840 GMT (4:40 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7780, up from S$1.7674 late Monday in Asia. It traded between S$1.7686 and S$1.7790. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened higher against its Singapore counterpart in Asia, in line with its overnight rise above Y120. It rose above S$1.7700 in early trades dealt out of Sydney, and extended its gains once Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market opened. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar broke successive resistance levels at S$1.7730 and S$1.7750, and revisited its Aug. 8 intraday peak of S$1.7790 by mid-afternoon. This followed the U.S. dollar's rise above 10,000 Indonesian rupiah. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah weakened after the Indonesian Supreme Court Monday nullified a guilty verdict against ex-President Suharto's son, Hutomo Mandala Putra, in a land fraud case. It was the U.S. dollar's first foray above IDR10,000 since July. \par \par \uc2  However, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  retreated from S$1.7790 amid fears that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the local dollar from sliding sharply. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, absence of any MAS action around S$1.7750 - the authority's last suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level - had emboldened market participants to push the U.S. dollar higher. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears to cap the U.S. dollar below S$1.7800 in overnight trading, with support at S$1.7740. \par \par \uc2  Singapore government bond prices rose Tuesday, mirroring overnight gains in U.S. Treasurys, as market participants positioned themselves for a likely 50-basis point cut in the U.S. federal-funds rate, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Federal Reserve's policy setting arm, the Federal Open Market Committee, is due to meet late Tuesday and is expected to cut rates again to boost the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  Local bond prices were also helped by softer interbank rates, which eased further amid flush liquidity in the banking system. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two- and five-year bonds ended down two basis points each at 1.88% and 2.89%, respectively. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds was down three basis points at 3.13%, while the yield on 10-year bonds fell two basis points to 3.38%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds fell one basis point to 3.73%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and\par are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be\par viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site:\par    \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, swaps were mostly lower, in line\par with local bond yields.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The most liquid seven-year swap offer closed two basis points lower at\par 3.25%, and the 10-year swap offer also closed two basis points lower, at\par 3.48%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps narrowed slightly to 112.5\par basis points from 113.5 basis points late Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interbank money market, rates were mostly lower, due to ample\par liquidity in the system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Tuesday      Late Monday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.1250% 0.3750%   0.1250% 0.2500\par 3-Month           1.6250% 1.7500%   1.6875% 1.8125%\par 1-Year            2.0000% 2.1250%   2.0625% 2.1875%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 415-4152;\par amit.prakashdowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011002dxa2000en\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
