\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Lower Fri, Tracking Weaker Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Barry Deely \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 999 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2001\par 12:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were lower late Friday in line with a decline in the yen, after the Bank of Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  once again in global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar, the Korean Won, the Indonesian rupiah and the New Taiwan dollar all closed lower against the dollar, while the Philippine peso bucked the trend, getting a moderate lift from the gradual flow of remittances made by overseas Filipino workers. \par \par \uc2  Market activity was fairly subdued ahead of the weekend, with many traders reluctant to sell dollars due to widespread notions the BOJ will continue to boost the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some traders also remained sidelined due to concerns the U.S. might launch a retaliatory strike in Afghanistan as part of its quest to punish the perpetrators of the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks in New York and Washington. \par \par \uc2  A late afternoon spurt of selling by Japanese banks and Hong Kong trading houses pushed the Singapore dollar lower. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  touched a high for the session at S$1.7675 before ending at S$1.7662, up from S$1.7613 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "There was some selling toward S$1.77 from some large Hong Kong houses," one trader said. He added that he expects the U.S. dollar to meet firm resistance at that level in the coming sessions, with the immediate trading range likely to fall between S$1.7580 and S$1.77. \par \par \uc2  In Seoul, dollar demand from domestic banks and oil importers keen to take care of their dollar needs ahead of a five-day weekend pushed the won lower. Korean financial markets will be closed Monday and Tuesday for Chuseok, the Korean Thanksgiving, and on Wednesday for National Foundation Day. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 1,309.6 won, up from Thursday's close of KRW1,306.4. \par \par \uc2  The tone for trading in most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was set in Tokyo, where monetary authorities continued their relentless efforts to cap the yen's upside. The BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared aimed at offsetting the effects of Japanese corporations repatriating dollar-assets to improve their balance sheets before closing their books at the Sept. 30 fiscal half year end. \par \par \uc2  The dollar strengthened initially against the yen following the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but later retreated to around Y118.90 late in the day, well off its Y119.75 high. \par \par \uc2  Some participants speculated that any further BOJ attempts to weaken the yen may meet with only limited success, as uncertainty in global financial markets is likely to discourage Japanese institutional investors from buying foreign assets. \par \par \uc2  "Given the global theme of risk aversion, it is extremely unlikely that we'll see any significant outflows of Japanese capital from October onwards," one trader said. \par \par \uc2  Although the BOJ was the only \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday, the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by other central banks in the region helped support some \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht weakened in light trade tracking the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, but fears of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of Thailand limited its slide, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 44.385 baht, up from THB44.305 baht at the Asian close Thursday, after moving in a narrow range between THB44.33 to THB44.39. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula's comments Wednesday fueled speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains ready to step into the market. He said the BOT would continue to prevent excess volatility in the baht's movements and would limit capital outflows. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Philippine peso also was supported in part on notions the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Although the dollar spiked early on expectations of month-end corporate requirements, thin demand and the gradual inflow from remittances made by overseas Filipino workers helped the peso gain some lost ground. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 51.32 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, down from PHP51.365 Friday and well below Friday's intraday high at PHP51.395. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah also ended lower as local companies continued to buy dollars amid fears the U.S. unit could rise amid growing anti-U.S. demonstrations. The dollar closed Asian trade at 9,710 rupiah, its intraday high, up from its close Thursday at IDR9,685. \par \par \uc2  Around 2,000 Muslims took to the streets of Jakarta Friday to protest possible U.S. retaliatory strikes against Afghanistan. Indonesia is the world's largest Muslim country, with around 90% of its 200 million population. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar ended nearly unchanged against its U.S. counterpart, coming off its earlier highs as market participants covered short U.S. dollar positions late in the session, dealers said. They also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of buying U.S. dollars to slow the local unit's rise. At 0800 GMT (4 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.549, nearly flat with Thursday's close of NT$34.548. \par \par \uc2  The market's focus next week will be directed toward Tuesday's Federal Open Market Committee meeting, at which the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is increasingly expected to cut interest rates by 25-50 basis points. \par \par \uc2  Also, traders also will monitor how the yen reacts to release Monday morning of the Bank of Japan's quarterly tankan business sentiment survey. This will be one of the first reports to show how terrorist attacks in the U.S. on Sept. 11 affected business sentiment in Japan, a senior government official said Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The tankan survey will be studied very carefully," Eiji Kawade, vice minister of the Cabinet Office, said in a regular press conference. "The attacks may have heightened risk factors in various forms" in Japan's business environment, he said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan will be release the tankan at 2350 GMT Sunday, or 8:50 a.m. Monday JST. \par \par \uc2  -By Barry Deely, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 415 4132; barry.deely@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010928dx9s000oj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
