\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late; Banks Unwind Long US Dlr Positions \b0\par\par\uc2 557 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2001\par 11:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was sharply higher late Thursday as banks unwound long U.S. dollar positions accumulated earlier this week, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0900 GMT (5 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7613, down from S$1.7690 late Wednesday. It traded between S$1.7588 and S$1.7693, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened nearly flat and drifted lower through most of the day, before being sold down sharply against the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar in late afternoon trading. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of Japan to weaken the yen briefly boosted the U.S. dollar against its Singapore counterpart, but the gains were short lived as local banks and interbank market participants resumed unwinding long U.S. dollar positions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Short of Singapore dollars, banks also sold yen and Thai baht crosses against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers added. \par \par \uc2  After having ended at S$1.7450 last week, the U.S. dollar surged to a seven-week high of S$1.7750 by early Wednesday and was viewed as overbought. Talk that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday to stem the U.S. dollar's steep rise sent dealers scurrying to unwind the positions. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's fall below the S$1.7630 support level triggered stop-loss selling which pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its intraday low of S$1.7588, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the U.S. dollar to find support at S$1.7550 in overnight trading. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds ended higher tracking overnight gains in U.S. Treasurys. Softer interbank rates also helped lift bond prices, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds fell four basis points to 1.88% while the yield on five-year bonds was down two basis points at 2.91%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds was down one basis point at 3.17% while the yield on 10-year bonds fell three basis points to 3.42%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds fell two basis points to 3.75%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs}}}\par \par \uc2  In the interest rate swaps market, swaps were mostly lower in line with some local bond yields. \par \par \uc2  The most liquid seven-year swap offer closed one basis point lower at 3.29% while the 10-year swap offer also closed one basis point lower at 3.53%. \par \par \uc2  The spread between two- and 10-year swaps widened to 115 basis points from 113 basis points late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  In the interbank money market, rates edged lower as DBS Bank joined the rate war, cutting its prime lending rate by 25 basis points to 4.75%, to become the cheapest lender in Singapore. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Thursday     Late Wednesday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.5000% 0.6250%   0.7500% 1.0000%\par 3-Month           1.7500% 1.8750%   1.8750% 2.0000%\par 1-Year            2.0625% 2.1875%   2.0625% 2.1875%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (