\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Asia: Singapore Dollar Options Volatilities Rise \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2 1,231 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2001\par 09:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Dollar bulls shouldn't be fooled into believing\par that the Singapore \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's precipitous decline to near seven-week\par lows heralds a reversal in the dollar-Sing dollar cross rate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  "I think it's a bit premature," said Thio Chin Loo, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas. "The market is in a risk-reduction mode." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar remains vulnerable to a knee-jerk selloff in the event of a military conflict, should the U.S. retaliate for the terrorist attacks in New York and Washington on Sept. 11., analysts say. \par \par \uc2  Further, with most economists expecting the terrorist assaults to drive the U.S. into recession - albeit a short one - and with Wall Street still shaky, the U.S. dollar is likely to continue tottering in the next few months even after knee-jerk selling in response to retaliatory raids runs its course, they add. \par \par \uc2  To be sure, recent activity in Singapore dollar trading would seem to defy this analysis, as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been rebounding against the local dollar. This move gained momentum Monday, as the dollar jumped 1.5% during the global trading day to as high as S$1.7725 in New York trade, from around S$1.7460 at the start of the Asian trading day. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar retreated to S$1.7643 around midday Tuesday on profit-taking by U.S. banks, dealers say. \par \par \uc2  "What happened last night was a little overdone," says a dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the move Monday was exacerbated by special factors. Traders say a number of market participants were forced to buy U.S. dollars and sell Singapore dollars in the cash market because they weren't positioned for a sharp move in the options market. \par \par \uc2  When banks sell options, they delta hedge to limit their exposure. That is, as the probability that an option will be exercised - Delta - rises, option writers reduce options exposure with an offsetting position in the cash market. \par \par \uc2  Banks can also buy gamma, which reflects the rate at which delta changes, for this purpose. A long gamma position benefits from a sudden rise in volatility. \par \par \uc2  In this case, banks that had sold dollar call options and Singapore dollar puts were suddenly forced to cover that exposure, either by buying dollars or outright gamma. \par \par \uc2  "The market is short gamma," says Conrad Kwok, chief options dealer at Singapore's DBS Bank. "People are looking to sell vega and buy gamma." \par \par \uc2  Vega measures the change in an option's price in relation to the change in volatility. \par \par \uc2  Volatilities implied by one-month U.S. dollar/Singapore dollar options jumped Tuesday to around 6.25%/7.50%, from around 4.25%/5.75% Monday. \par \par \uc2  One-year options implied volatility of 5.50%/6.75% Tuesday, higher than 4.75%/6.50% Monday. \par \par \uc2  Volatility measures the expected range in the value of an asset over the period of the contract. Volatilities on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options gauge the anticipated moves in the underlying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with a higher volatility implying a wider trading range. \par \par \uc2  The shift toward becoming risk averse has encouraged options writers to hedge exposure but hasn't spurred a major move by end users. One reason is the sudden costliness of these instruments. The steep fall in the Singapore dollar Monday has also pushed bid-offer spreads in volatilities far apart, making it difficult to trade and expensive for buyers. \par \par \uc2  "People have marked the vol curve higher, but not much is seen in the way of trading, at least in the broker market," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options specialist at a U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  "People don't want to pay such high vols to buy," he adds. "And they don't want to short vols because there's too much risk - right now, they could have a war or another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in dollar/yen which would affect the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  received an added fillip Monday after Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying dollars for yen even though Japanese markets were closed for a holiday, and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought euros against yen on behalf of Japan. The yen's decline added to the pressure on the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  Speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore had bought the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Friday, pushing it above S$1.7400, also made participants realize that it would be futile to push the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  much lower. \par \par \uc2  Though many \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  participants are betting for a depreciation of the Singapore dollar to around S$1.8000 on a six-month horizon, the appetite for Singapore dollar puts has been measly, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Whatever trades that were done, six-to-12 month maturity Singapore dollar put options with strike prices ranging from S$1.7800 to S$1.8500 were popular. \par \par \uc2  In the near term, analysts expect the Singapore dollar to stay resilient, finding support from the city-state's healthy current account surplus and the yen's strength even in the face of further possible rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. dollar's bounce is in response to actions of central banks and the market in risk reduction mode," said Thio Chin Loo, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  "Once military confrontation starts, investors will be prepared to put new positions by shorting the U.S. dollar," she adds. \par \par \uc2  Though Thio says the U.S. dollar could technically extend its rebound toward S$1.7850 resistance, and possibly as far as S$1.7975, "barring a breach of 1.8000, the bear trend is intact." \par \par \uc2  She adds that she expects the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to eventually retreat to S$1.7200, and possibly form a base around S$1.7000. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is then expected to rebound to S$1.8000 in about six months' time, with U.S. economic growth widely expected to resume in the first quarter of 2002, helped by aggressive interest rate cuts and a big boost in federal spending. \par \par \uc2  The threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar has also ebbed, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar now trading in the weaker half of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's policy band, providing some comfort to the authorities, some analysts say. The fallout of the attacks in the U.S. on the global economy is expected to aggravate demand for Singapore's exports. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had intermittently bought U.S. dollars to curb the appreciation of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the wake of the Sept. 11 attacks, as the Singapore dollar traded in the stronger half of the MAS' band. \par \par \uc2  "On a trade-weighted basis, the Singapore dollar has weakened about 2% in the last one week," says Jimmy Koh, the head of treasury research at UOB Group. "It's at the weaker half of the band, but well within the band, so there's no need for the MAS to do anything." \par \par \uc2  The MAS uses the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than interest rates, as its primary monetary-policy tool in seeking to maintain brisk growth and low inflation. The Singapore dollar is guided within a trading band against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the country's major trading partners. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4807;\par netty.ismail@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4258 5095 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010925dx9p000fj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
