\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Dlr Fails To Sustain Rise \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 740 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2001\par 11:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed late Thursday, after having recouped most of their earlier losses as the dollar remained wobbly against the yen despite the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities. \par \par \uc2  With little in the form of direction from the stable yen in early trading, most of the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell under the sway of the Singapore dollar's swift decline, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But the U.S. dollar failed to sustain its upward momentum, eventually succumbing to worries over the economic fallout of last week's terrorist attacks and uncertainty over the nature and extent of U.S. retaliation against the perpetrators of the assaults, dealers said. The dollar's fate is also tied to the performance of the U.S. equities market, which remains shaky. \par \par \uc2  "Everything should be sideways, at least between now and the end of the month," said a dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  As the U.S. gears up for war, Islamic clerics Thursday urged terrorist suspect Osama bin Laden to voluntarily leave Afghanistan, where he and his followers have had sanctuary for five years, the Taliban news agency said. \par \par \uc2  The clerics' statement set no deadline for bin Laden to accept or reject the call, and it was unclear whether this would be enough to dissuade U.S. President George W. Bush from launching massive military strikes against Afghanistan. \par \par \uc2  Fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if not talk of actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also kept the market nervous, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Central banks galore," said the European bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso and the Indonesian rupiah regained their footing after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of having sold dollars to halt the depreciation of their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers believed Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought U.S. dollars to prevent the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening with the yen. \par \par \uc2  There was also some talk - which many in the market played down, if not dismissed - that the authorities in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Thailand had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Early in the day, the Singapore dollar floundered to S$1.7460 against its U.S. counterpart, compared with S$1.7417 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  U.S. dollar purchases by a large U.S. bank and investment houses based in Hong Kong propelled the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. The sharp jump fueled speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore had bought U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  But profit-taking subsequently led to a retreat in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At 0935 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.7416, little changed from\par S$1.7417 late Wednesday.\par - - 20/09/01 09-48G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The South Korean won and the New Taiwan dollar teetered as foreign\par funds bailed out of their equity markets, dealers said. The stock market\par in Seoul slid by 1.3%, while the Taipei stock index skidded by 2.2%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The New Taiwan dollar, however, found respite from U.S. dollar\par purchases by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  late in the session, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The New Taiwan dollar ended at NT$34.575 against the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par marginally weaker compared with Wednesday's close of $34.572. Trading\par volume was US$279.0 million, less than half of Wednesday's US$719.0\par million.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Seoul, the dollar continued to face strong resistance around 1,300\par won. The dollar finished at 1,297.5 won, slightly higher than KRW1,296.5\par Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Foreigners bought about $100 million worth of dollars and are expected\par to buy more in the coming days as they have unloaded over KRW330 billion\par in shares so far this week, a Seoul-based dealer said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Thai \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakened to 44.220 baht per dollar, compared with\par THB44.190 late Tuesday as U.S. banks snapped up dollars, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Indonesian rupiah rebounded, as reports of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar\par sales prompted foreign banks to lock in profits, dealers said. Dollar\par demand by local companies needing to repay their debt or fund their\par import requirements earlier sent the Indonesian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reeling to\par 9,735 rupiah against the dollar, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Late Thursday, the dollar had retreated to IDR9,445, compared with\par IDR9,630 late Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the Philippine Dealing System, the dollar closed at 51.290 pesos,\par down from PHP51.400 Wednesday.\par \f28 \p