\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Mkt Awaits Word On Bin Laden \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2001\par 12:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed against the dollar Wednesday as investors digested the latest developments in the U.S. effort to force the extradition of suspected terrorist Osama Bin Laden. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, which has suffered in recent weeks due mainly to the deteriorating U.S. economy, got a boost from the Bank of Japan, which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the second time this week in an attempt to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar, Philippine peso and Indonesian rupiah all tumbled against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Thai baht edged higher, while the South Korean won and New Taiwan dollar were little changed. \par \par \uc2  However, the outlook for the dollar over the medium term remains poor, analysts said, citing fears the U.S. economy may tip into recession and the difficulty the country will have in funding its ballooning current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  Last week's terrorist attacks in New York and Washington, and the threat of a U.S. military response have also hurt sentiment toward the dollar and U.S. stocks. \par \par \uc2  President Bush has demanded Afghanistan's hard-line Islamic Taliban government turn over Bid Laden, who is suspected of masterminding the attacks. Taliban leaders were considering whether to extradite Bid Laden or declare a holy war on the U.S. if it attacks Afghanistan. \par \par \uc2  An easing of tensions between the two sides Wednesday apparently soothed the markets and lent support to the dollar, said Thio Chin Loo, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  "Today things were a little calmer than usual. Bush toned down his rhetoric and Afghanistan's leaders were more conciliatory," she said. \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, the BOJ purchased dollars in the open market on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an attempt to put a floor under the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The move pushed the dollar from an intraday low of Y117.10 to a high of Y118.12. However, by late in the day the dollar had slipped to 117.54, down from Y117.94 late Tuesday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said Japan was in close contact with U.S. and European authorities when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday, though Japan acted alone, and warned that the ministry was ready to take steps again if needed. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar tracked the yen lower after the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But unlike the yen, it didn't rebound as the day wore on. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7417, up from S$1.7361 late Monday. The U.S. dollar drifted up to S$1.7420 before buying fizzled out. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the reluctance to take fresh positions stemmed from uncertainty over how the U.S. might respond to last week's terrorist attacks. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar ended little changed as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought U.S. dollars shortly before the market closed to offset the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's early gains, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar closed at NT$34.572 against the U.S. dollar, compared with $34.575 Friday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was closed Monday and Tuesday due to a tropical storm. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won also was barely changed from Tuesday's close. The dollar finished at KRW1,296.5 won, down slightly from KRW1,296.9 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The won fell in tandem with the yen after the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Importer buying of dollars, which picked up in the wake of the BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also weighed on the won. \par \par \uc2  However, the won managed to trim its losses as the yen climbed back later in the session. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah weakened on corporate demand for dollars, but closed off its day lows as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently sold dollars to help the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted between 9,590 rupiah and IDR9,640 late Wednesday, much higher than IDR9,520 to IDR9,560 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso was pressured by corporate demand for dollars and losses in some regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at PHP51.400 on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP51.370 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht drew support from foreign stock buying, but gains were capped by exporter dollar buying. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the dollar was quoted at 44.190 baht, down from THB44.225 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010919dx9j000ot\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
