\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late On Broad US Dlr Weakness;Thin Trade \b0\par\par\uc2 645 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2001\par 10:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was higher in very thin trading late Monday, in line with the U.S. dollar's broader weakness against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0755 GMT (3:55 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7335, down from S$1.7376 late Friday in Asia. According to dealers, it traded between S$1.7310 and S$1.7335. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened around its intraday high against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reflecting losses against the yen and euro late Friday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Around midday, the U.S. dollar drifted lower to its intraday low, in step with its brief fall below Y117. \par \par \uc2  Subsequently, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of Japan boosted the U.S. dollar against the yen and nudged it slightly higher against the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar as well, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't do much for the dollar/Sing because the market is still nervous about the (U.S.) dollar ahead of the reopening of the U.S. stock market today," said a senior dealer at a European bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said trading was very thin with most of the trades being dealt through brokers. \par \par \uc2  "There weren't any interbank calls," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Market participants expect the U.S. stock market to open sharply lower when it reopens later Monday, following Tuesday's terrorist strikes in New York and Washington. This would pressure the U.S. dollar lower. \par \par \uc2  Adding to the uncertainly about the U.S. dollar are expectations among many market participants that the Federal Reserve may cut U.S. interest rates before the U.S. stock markets reopen. There are also fears that a sharp slide in the U.S. dollar may prompt coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by major central banks. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the U.S. dollar to trade between S$1.7270 and S$1.7370 over the next few hours and then in a wider S$1.7250-S$1.7400 range overnight. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds were higher Monday after a rally in U.S. Treasurys overnight and on expectations of a sharp fall in U.S. stocks when U.S. equities market reopen later Monday. \par \par \uc2  A sharp fall in the local stock market and soft local interbank money rates also helped bond prices, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds was down five basis points at 2.05% and the yield on five-year bonds was down four basis points at 2.73%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds was down one basis point at 3.18% while the yield on 10-year bonds was down three basis points at 3.41%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds was down half a basis point at 3.715%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, swap offers edged lower tracking\par bond yields.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The most liquid seven-year swap offer closed down two basis points at\par 3.33% while the 10-year swap offer closed one basis point lower at\par 3.55%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps widened to 99 basis points\par from 98 basis points late Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interbank money market, rates were steady amid flush liquidity\par in the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Monday       Late Friday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.2500% 0.5000%   0.2500% 0.5000%\par 3-Month           2.0000% 2.1250%   2.0000% 2.1250%\par 1-Year            2.1875% 2.3125%   2.1875% 2.3125%\par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 415-4152;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010917dx9h02ip6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
