\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Up Late As Terrorist Attacks Bruise Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 839 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2001\par 10:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  received a boost late Wednesday, after Tuesday's terrorist attacks in the U.S. sent the dollar reeling in global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and threatened to smash the already fragile confidence in the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  But trading was extremely thin as shell-shocked participants largely stayed on the sidelines, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The interbank market in Singapore ground to a halt, leaving participants to trade via brokers and electronic broking systems, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "There's nothing we much we can do," said a dealer at a European bank. "Liquidity is bad. If we're holding big positions, we're just trying to get out slowly." \par \par \uc2  Several central banks in the region - including authorities in Japan, Singapore and Korea - early in the day assured the markets they were prepared to offer additional liquidity to the banking system. \par \par \uc2  Position-squaring dominated trading, as market participants focused on ensuring transaction settlements were going through smoothly. Dealers said there were no reports of any major hiccups so far. \par \par \uc2  Still, "everybody's concerned whether or not the next few sessions we'll see any settlement problems," said a dealer a U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  The cloud of uncertainty is expected to persist, and the dollar likely to remain wobbly, as observers consider the economic implications of the attacks in New York and Washington and wonder about how the U.S. government would retaliate. \par \par \uc2  Hijacked planes destroyed New York's two tallest buildings, and crashed into the Pentagon, badly damaging the headquarters of the U.S. Department of Defense. \par \par \uc2  "We don't want to be opportunistic to take advantage of the situation and hammer the U.S. dollar. Emotionally it's wrong, politically it's wrong, even though economically, it's right," said a dealer at a European bank. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  participants were bracing for a collapse in U.S. stocks when the equity market resumes trading, expected Thursday, dealers said. Trade was suspended Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Following the attacks, the U.S. Federal Reserve is expected to act swiftly and aggressively to calm financial markets and cushion their blow on the economy, market watchers said. Already, talk of emergency rate cuts of at least 25 basis points, as early as this week, has swirled around the market. \par \par \uc2  But such expectations didn't quell jitters of a likely downdraft in U.S. stocks anticipated Thursday, with most Asian stock markets tumbling Wednesday. Stock markets in Kuala Lumpur and Bangkok were closed Wednesday following the overnight attacks. The impact of the region's equity losses on the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was, however, muted, with participants focusing on the dollar's fate, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Barclays Capital said in a research note that while it is too early to conclude definitively on the likely economic and policy implications of Tuesday's carnage, by the same token, "the widespread talk of a global recession and sustained global financial crisis does appear premature, based on current information." \par \par \uc2  Barclays said the dollar's downtrend against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  "could prove temporary," with the Japanese authorities threatening to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's strengthening, and amid fears that Asia's balance of payments would suffer from reduced capital inflows and higher oil prices. \par \par \uc2  Fears of, if not actual, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the region's monetary authorities pulled Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  off their intraday highs, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers believed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  pulled the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar off its nine-month high of S$1.7260, set overnight following the attacks on the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers suspected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again Wednesday in Asia, when the U.S. dollar was quoted around S$1.7340. \par \par \uc2  At 0937 GMT (5:37 a.m. EDT), the Singapore dollar was quoted at S$1.7376 against its U.S. counterpart, stronger than S$1.7539 late Tuesday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought an estimated US$800 million - around US$300 million in the last few minutes of trading - to push up the U.S. dollar from the intraday low of NT$34.403, said a dealer at a foreign bank in Taipei. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$34.599, down from NT$34.684 Tuesday. Turnover swelled to US$1.27 billion from Tuesday's US$338.5 million. \par \par \uc2  In Seoul, the Finance and Economy Ministry said it would go all out "to ensure market stability through various measures if the won moves sharply from investors' uneasiness." \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 1,286.1 won, down from Tuesday's close of KRW1,295.8. \par \par \uc2  On the Philippine Dealing System, the dollar closed at 51.230 pesos, down from PHP51.300 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was stronger at 44.435 baht per dollar, compared with THB44.810 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at 9,060 rupiah per dollar, firmer compared with IDR9,095 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4043; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010912dx9c000n0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
