\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Up Late On Yen; Baht Leads Rise \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2001\par 11:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  received a boost from the yen's vigor late Friday, with the Thai baht leading the rise as it snapped out of its recent range to trade at five-month highs. \par \par \uc2  With the yen still ambling in familiar territory, emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are expected to remain in limbo in the near term, observers said. \par \par \uc2  "There's no clear view, no real trend to pick," said a dealer at a European bank. "You can't go long Asians given the weak fundamentals." \par \par \uc2  At 0855 GMT (3:20 a.m. EDT), the dollar was quoted at Y119.80, little changed from Y119.78 late Thursday in New York, but lower than Y120.31 late Thursday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht grabbed the limelight, as dollar sales by offshore U.S. banks and investment houses triggered stop-loss orders when the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  broke below THB44.500 and finally THB44.400 - a critical support level that had held up for the most part of day, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday, the dollar was quoted at 44.315 baht, its lowest level since end-March. Late Thursday, the dollar was much higher at THB44.630. \par \par \uc2  As the baht found support from the yen and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, the absence of much-feared \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  emboldened offshore participants to unwind their long dollar positions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Thai corporates also appeared to have pulled out their bids for dollars from the market, removing a source of support for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Onshore corporates are net long dollars," said the dealer at the European bank. "It will take a long time before they come in to buy, maybe at around THB44." \par \par \uc2  To a lesser extent, dealers also attributed the baht's advance to remarks by Finance Minister Somkid Jatusripitak that the Government of Singapore Investment Corp. has expressed an initial interest in setting up a joint fund with the Thai government. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra had suggested to his Singapore counterpart, Goh Chok Tong, during a visit this week to Singapore that the Government of Singapore Investment Corp. consider jointly investing with his government in a Thai equities fund. \par \par \uc2  The equities fund would try to draw foreign investment by matching it with government contributions. \par \par \uc2  The apparent receptiveness to the idea has propelled Thailand's benchmark stock index by 2.3% Friday. \par \par \uc2  Against the Singapore dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  skirted around the S$1.7500 level, failing to make much headway below that level, even when the U.S. dollar broke below the critical THB44.400 support against its Thai counterpart, dealers said. Market participants were wary of getting the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's hackles up, they added. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7498, lower than S$1.7560 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Exporter dollar sales combined with the yen's resilience to prop up the South Korean won and the New Taiwan dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But foreign equity outflows - the Taipei stock index plunged 3.4% - and U.S. dollar purchases by Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wiped out a bulk of the New Taiwan dollar's earlier gains, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  In early trade, the New Taiwan dollar hit an intraday high of NT$34.596 against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar ended at NT$34.625 per U.S. dollar, marginally stronger compared with NT$34.629 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  In Seoul, the dollar finished at 1,281.20 won, down from KRW1,283.80 Thursday. The threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tempered the won's advance, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was slightly stronger at 8,690 rupiah per dollar, compared with IDR8,725 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Corporate dollar demand dented the Philippine peso slightly, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 51.050 pesos, a shade higher than PHP51.000 Thursday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4043;\par netty.ismail@dowjones.com\par -0- 24/08/01 09-38G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010824dx8o01jjr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
