\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late, Tracks Yen; Local Bk Sells US Dlrs \b0\par\par\uc2 645 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2001\par 10:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was higher late Friday, as market participants squared long U.S. dollar positions ahead of the weekend given the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness against the yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0819 GMT (4:19 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7498, down from S$1.7559 late Thursday in Asia. It traded between S$1.7485 and S$1.7535. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened lower against its Singapore counterpart in Asia, in line with its move below Y120. It drifted lower before the start of local interbank trading in Singapore on selling by Sydney-based market participants, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  During the local session, the U.S. dollar drifted lower in line with its losses against the yen, and fell below S$1.7500 when a large local bank squared its long U.S. dollar positions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, market participants had feared that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the local unit's strength if the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell below S$1.7500. \par \par \uc2  But such fears ebbed once the local bank sold U.S. dollars, dealers said. A U.S. bank also squared its U.S. dollar longs, they added. \par \par \uc2  Later Friday, in London and New York, the Singapore dollar is expected to continue mirroring the yen's moves. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is expected to find initial support at S$1.7480 and then at S$1.7450, with resistance around S$1,7530, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds ended lower Friday as market participants squared their positions ahead of Monday's yield auction of the new S$2.2 billion 15-year government bond, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Selling of the new bond in the gray market also nudged participants to sell benchmark bonds, a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  In the gray market, the new 15-year bond was quoted on a when-issued basis at yields of 3.85% to 3.85%, compared with 3.84% to 3.81% Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts say that the when-issued yields appear to be consolidating around current levels, and that the average auction yield could come in around 3.85%. \par \par \uc2  Applications for the new bond close at 0400 GMT Monday (midnight EDT) and the result of the yield auction is expected around 0830 GMT (4:30 a.m. EDT). The bond will be issued Sept. 3. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds was flat at 2.21%, while the yield on five-year bonds rose one basis point to 2.90%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds rose one basis point to 3.31%, while the 10-year bond yield rose one basis point to 3.58%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, swaps tracked bond yields and were\par flat to higher.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The most liquid seven-year swap offer closed two basis points higher\par at 3.49%, while the 10-year swap offer closed flat at 3.71%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two- and 10-year was unchanged from Thursday at\par 107 basis points.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interbank money market, term rates held flat, while the\par overnight rate drifted lower amid improved liquidity.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Friday       Late Thursday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight        1.7500% 1.8750%   2.0000% 2.1250%\par 3-Month          2.2500% 2.3750%   2.2500% 2.3750%\par 1-Year           2.2500% 2.3750%   2.2500% 2.3750%\par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4152;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par -0- 24/08/01 08-49G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010824dx8o01jhi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
