\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Higher Late On Yen; MAS Rumored Buying Dlrs \b0\par\par\uc2 573 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2001\par 10:42\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar rose sharply late Thursday as foreign banks and investors sold U.S. dollars in the wake of a soaring yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The local dollar shook off several rounds of suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at smoothening its sharp rise, they added. \par \par \uc2  An MAS spokesperson wasn't immediately able to comment on the suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At 0821 GMT (4:21 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7430, down from S$1.7533 late Wednesday in Asia. It traded between S$1.7420 and S$1.7515. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar opened higher compared with Wednesday's Asian close, in line with the yen's rise above Y120 per U.S. dollar and further gains in the euro, although it was off its overnight high around S$1.7400. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said U.S. dollar buying by local banks, suspected to have acted as MAS' agents, forced the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to retreat from its overnight high. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar opened around S$1.7470, rose to S$1.7440 in early trade, then fell sharply to S$1.7520 on the yen's brief slide to below Y120 per U.S. dollar. Local banks and a Swiss bank were the main sellers of Singapore dollars for U.S. dollars in this round, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Then, during thin lunch-time trade, U.S. dollar sales by a European bank once again boosted the Singapore dollar to S$1.7440. This, again, attracted local banks to buy U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  In late afternoon trade, the Singapore dollar hit its intraday high of S$1.7420, tracking the yen's rise to Y119.29 versus the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the U.S. dollar to find support at S$1.7400 overnight, then at S$1.7350, while its upside is expected to be capped at S$1.7520. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds were mostly flat in thin trading and as interbank interest rates edged higher, curbing the market's appetite for bonds. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds rose one basis point to 2.21% while the yield on five-year bonds was flat at 2.90%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yields on seven- and 10-year bonds were both flat at 3.31% and 3.58%, respectively. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and\par are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be\par viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site:\par    \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, swaps were mostly flat, in line\par with local bond yields.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The most liquid seven-year swap offers closed flat at 3.47%, while the\par 10-year swap offer closed one basis point higher at 3.72%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two- and 10-year swap widened to 108 basis\par points from 107 basis points late Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interbank money market, rates edged higher amid tighter\par liquidity.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Thursday     Late Wednesday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight        2.4375% 2.5625%   2.1250% 2.2500%\par 3-Month          2.2500% 2.3750%   2.1875% 2.3125%\par 1-Year           2.2500% 2.3750%   2.2500% 2.3750%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4152;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010816dx8g000g0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
